{"kind":"generation","id":"probability","model":"google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite","status":200,"pass":false,"ms":1499,"text":"Let R represent drawing a red ball and B represent drawing a blue ball.\nThe total number of balls in the bag is $3$ red + $2$ blue = $5$ balls.\n\nWe are drawing two balls without replacement. We want to find the probability that both balls drawn are red.\n\nThe probability of drawing a red ball on the first draw is the number of red balls divided by the total number of balls.\nP(R1) = $\\frac{\\text{Number of red balls}}{\\text{Total number of balls}} = \\frac{3}{5}$\n\nAfter the first draw, if a red ball was drawn, there are now $3 - 1 = 2$ red balls left and the total number of balls in the bag is $5 - 1 = 4$.\n\nThe probability of drawing a red ball on the second draw, given that the first ball drawn was red, is the number of remaining red balls divided by the total number of remaining balls.\nP(R2 | R1) = $\\frac{\\text{Number of remaining red balls}}{\\text{Total number of remaining balls}} = \\frac{2}{4}$\n\nThe probability that both balls drawn are red is the product of the probability of drawing a red ball on the first draw and the probability of drawing a red ball on the second draw given that the first was red.\nP(R1 and R2) = P(R1) * P(R2 | R1)\nP(R1 and R2) = $\\frac{3}{5} \\times \\frac{2}{4}$\n\nNow, we multiply the fractions:\nP(R1 and R2) = $\\frac{3 \\times 2}{5 \\times 4} = \\frac{6}{20}$\n\nFinally, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms. The greatest common divisor of 6 and 20 is 2.\n$\\frac{6}{20} = \\frac{6 \\div 2}{20 \\div 2} = \\frac{3}{10}$\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{3/10}$.","usage":{"prompt_tokens":30,"completion_tokens":452,"total_tokens":482,"cost":0.0001838,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.0001838,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.000003,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.